elegation.md\nskill/references/image-models.md\nskill/references/lifecycle.md\nskill/references/project-role-selection.md\nskill/references/single-source-deployment.md\nskill/references/two-box-capability-matrix.md\nskill/scripts/route_check.py\nskill/scripts/tiiny-swap.sh\nskill/scripts/tiiny_ctl.py\nskill/scripts/tiiny_two_box_matrix.py\nskill/templates/work-order.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.hermes-plugin-package-01-prune-repo-and-move-package-to-root/OpenHuman/README.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":13,"newStart":0,"newLines":0,"lines":["-[# OpenHuman","-","-Community-authored articles and guides for the OpenHuman agent platform.","-","-## Articles","-","-- **openhuman-reinstall-guide.md** — Step-by-step guide for reinstalling OpenHuman on macOS without losing your memory tree. Includes a one-script automation for advanced users.","-","-- **community-docx-workflow.md** — How to feed  files directly to your OpenHuman agent via the workspace folder, bypassing the need for PDF/OCR conversion.","-","----","-","-*Contributions welcome — open an issue or PR to add articles.*"]}],"deleted":true},{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.hermes-plugin-package-01-prune-repo-and-move-package-to-root/OpenHuman/community-docx-workflow.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":116,"newStart":0,"newLines":0,"lines":["-# Feeding .docx Files to Your OpenHuman Agent","-","-**By Gary Garcia and Agent 99 · August 11, 2026 · v1.0**","-","-**Problem:** The OpenHuman UI has no method for uploading a `.docx` file directly to your agent. Users end up converting to PDF and relying on OCR, which wastes tokens and loses formatting.","-","-**Solution:** OpenHuman's agent workspace *is a folder on disk*. Drop a `.docx` into that folder, and your agent can read and edit it natively — no PDF, no OCR, formatting preserved.","-","-## The Mental Model","-","-OpenHuman agents read files from their workspace, which is a real folder on your machine. Anything you put there is something your agent can open. The `docx` skill simply removes the PDF/OCR fallback for Word documents — the agent reads the `.docx` directly.","-","-## Setup (one-time)","-","-### 1. Link the projects folder to your desktop (recommended)","-","-OpenHuman's UI lives on your desktop — that's where you work. The fastest way to get files into your agent is to make the projects folder appear right there, so you can drag files in without keeping Finder open.","-","-Create a symlink (or Finder alias) from your desktop to the OpenHuman projects folder:","-","-On macOS, in Terminal:","-","-```bash","-ln -s \"<path to your OpenHuman projects folder>\" ~/Desktop/OpenHuman-Projects","-```","-","-> **Where is the projects folder?** It's the agent's workspace root. On this setup it lives under iCloud Drive (e.g. `~/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/OpenHuman/projects/`). Confirm the exact path by asking your agent for its working directory.","-","-### 2. Install the docx skill","-","-Install the `docx` skill from the skill registry (it's the Nous Research / built-in one). Your agent can do this for you, or you can install it yourself.","-","-## The Workflow","-","-1. **Drop** the `.docx` you want to work with onto the desktop symlink (or into the projects folder directly).","-2. **Tell your agent** to find and edit it, e.g. \"Open `annual-report.docx` in the documents folder and change X to Y.\"","-3. **The agent** finds the file in its workspace, reads it natively, makes the edit, and saves in place.","-4. **You get back** the same `.docx` with formatting intact — bold, italic, styles, tables all preserved.","-","-## What Works","-","-- **Native reading** — no PDF conversion, no OCR, far fewer tokens.","-- **Formatting preserved** — bold, italic, and styles survive edits.","-- **In-place editing** — the agent edits the file directly; no re-upload needed.","-- **Repeatable** — a helper CLI (`docx_edit.py`) supports read / replace / append for scripted or batch edits.","-","-## Troubleshooting","-","-- **\"I can't find the file\"** — the file must be inside the agent's workspace folder, not just anywhere on disk. The desktop symlink resolves to that folder, so dropping on the symlink works — but a file sitting loose on the desktop (outside the symlink) does not.","-- **Path restrictions** — agents can't read arbitrary paths outside their workspace (e.g. `~/.openhuman/...`). Always drop files into the workspace folder.","-- **Formatting looks off** — if an edit spans multiple runs of text, the agent may apply the formatting of the first run it touches. For complex edits, ask the agent to verify formatting after saving.","-","-## Disclaimer","-","-This solution has been tested by one person on a Mac running OpenHuman, using the built-in `docx` skill and a `python-docx` helper. The concept has not been tested on Windows or Linux, though it should work there in principle.","-","-This solution has **not** been tested by the OpenHuman team and is **not supported by them**. The file-handling and skill systems in OpenHuman have changed more than once in recent months, so the ability to self-support this solution matters.","-","-**No warranty is offered or implied.** This is a workflow, not a product. If you read this and don't understand how it works, do not attempt it. Edits are made in place to your original file, so if you don't understand how your environment differs, back up your documents first.","-","-## Why This Matters","-","-This pattern isn't just about `.docx` — it's the general trick for getting any file into your agent's hands when the UI won't let you upload it. Drop it in the workspace, reference it by name, and let the agent's native file tools do the rest.","-","-*Feeding .docx Files to Your OpenHuman Agent · v1.0 · August 11, 2026 · Gary Garcia and Agent 99*","-","----","-","-## Enabling the Subconscious Loop (config.toml)","-","-The OpenHuman UI toggle for the subconscious loop doesn't always persist. If you flip it on, restart, and the loop still isn't running, edit the config file directly.","-","-**File to edit:**","-`~/.openhuman/users/<USER-ID>/config.toml`","-","-(Confirm your exact path by asking your agent for its data directory. This guide is written against schema_version 6.)","-","-**The real gates** are the `local_ai` runtime flags and the `*_provider` fields. The legacy `[local_ai.usage]` booleans are preset/migration-only and do NOT override the provider fields after migration.","-","-**Edits (current value → new value):**","-","-In the `[local_ai]` section — turn the local runtime on:","-```toml","-runtime_enabled = false   →   runtime_enabled = true","-opt_in_confirmed = false  →   opt_in_confirmed = true","-```","-","-Fix the subconscious provider — it must point at a CHAT model, not an embedding model:","-```toml","-subconscious_provider = \"ollama:bge-m3\"   →   subconscious_provider = \"ollama:gemma3:1b-it-qat\"","-```","-(`bge-m3` is an embedding model. The subconscious loop is a chat/reasoning workload, so it needs a chat model like `gemma3:1b-it-qat`.)","-","-In the `[heartbeat]` section — enable the tick loop (there is no `subconscious_mode` key in this schema; the heartbeat block is what fires ticks):","-```toml","-enabled = false              →   enabled = true","-inference_enabled = false    →   inference_enabled = true","-```","-","-**Optional — route embeddings and the memory tree fully local** (only if you want on-device, not cloud):","-```toml","-[memory]","-embedding_provider = \"cloud\"   →   embedding_provider = \"ollama\"","-embedding_model = \"embedding-v1\"   →   embedding_model = \"bge-m3\"","-","-[memory_tree]","-llm_backend = \"cloud\"   →   llm_backend = \"local\"","-```","-","-**After editing:** save the file, then fully quit and relaunch OpenHuman (not just a window close — the daemon needs to re-read config on boot).","-","-**Also check:** the models must be pulled in Ollama: `gemma3:1b-it-qat` (chat) and `bge-m3` (embeddings), or the loop won't have a model to run on.","-","-**Verify:** after restart, check **Intelligence → Subconscious** in the UI (or `openhuman.inference_status` via CLI) to confirm the loop is enabled and ticking.","-","----"]}],"deleted":true},{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.hermes-plugin-package-01-prune-repo-and-move-package-to-root/README.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":61,"newStart":0,"newLines":0,"lines":["-# AI_Articles","-","-Published articles and essays on AI tools, agent patterns, and productivity systems.","-","-By [Gary Garcia](https://github.com/garciacionet-alt) · `#TheStrategicTechnicalAlliancesGuy`","-","-[![XERJ Corpus Hub contributor](https://hub.xerj.org/contributors/garciacionet-alt/badge.svg)](https://hub.xerj.org/contributors/garciacionet-alt)","-","----","-","-## What this repo is","-","-A small, personal publication of reference patterns and practical essays for people using AI agents to do real work. Each article ships with a portable pattern description and a concrete implementation guide.","-","-Articles are written from the practitioner side — after running the pattern, not before.","-","-## Articles","-","-| Article | What it covers | Pattern | Implementation |","-|---|---|---|---|","-| [Agent Memory Commons](./agent-memory-commons/) | A shared file space that lets multiple AI agents (Hermes, Claude Code, Minimax M3, OpenCode, OpenHuman, custom openclaw) share context through a synced Obsidian vault — no coordination overhead, no API contracts — plus the operating layer that keeps it legible: a root protocol file, a closed frontmatter vocabulary, a read-only linter, live views, and a session-end ritual, and an optional version-control add-on for keeping a history of the vault's own structure | [PATTERN.md](./agent-memory-commons/PATTERN.md) | [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./agent-memory-commons/IMPLEMENTATION.md) |","-| [Bot Identity](./bot-identity/) | One credential per bot. Every framework makes multi-agent easy; almost none tell you to give each bot its own channel token and OAuth grant — and without one you cannot scope, revoke, or attribute anything to a specific agent | [PATTERN.md](./bot-identity/PATTERN.md) | [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./bot-identity/IMPLEMENTATION.md) |","-| [Tiiny Model Routing](./tiiny-model-routing/) | Routing delegated subtasks to the right model on a local appliance — the tier table, the NPU budget that constrains it, a delegation backend that routes per task, and a swap script that survives the watchdog | [README.md](./tiiny-model-routing/README.md) | [PATTERN.md](./tiiny-model-routing/PATTERN.md) · [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./tiiny-model-routing/IMPLEMENTATION.md) |","-| [Tiiny Two Boxes](./tiiny-two-boxes/) | Running two local appliances as one system — the per-device credential, address and lock a second box needs, the vault/flex role split that stops the boxes evicting each other, and the failures that look like hardware problems but are not | [README.md](./tiiny-two-boxes/README.md) | [PATTERN.md](./tiiny-two-boxes/PATTERN.md) · [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./tiiny-two-boxes/IMPLEMENTATION.md) |","-| [TiinyMesh](./tiiny-mesh/) | A multi-target inference gateway and control plane — turns multiple local appliances, Mac desktop models, and cloud providers into a single OpenAI-compatible endpoint with automated model borrowing, Agent Memory Commons health witnessing, OpenRouter credit tracking, and an in-flight cloud fallback toggle | [README.md](./tiiny-mesh/README.md) | [PATTERN.md](./tiiny-mesh/PATTERN.md) · [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./tiiny-mesh/IMPLEMENTATION.md) |","-| [Tiiny Image Agency](./tiiny-image-agency/) | An image-generation API on a local appliance — words become a requirement vector, a deterministic resolver picks the model and explains itself, residency is managed as one transaction against a shared NPU pool, and a browser surface shows what the device is doing. Ships the whole application, its 465 tests, and the skill. **Built on macOS; a Linux or Windows port is wanted and is three known edits away** | [README.md](./tiiny-image-agency/README.md) | [PATTERN.md](./tiiny-image-agency/PATTERN.md) · [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./tiiny-image-agency/IMPLEMENTATION.md) |","-| [Remote Agent Access](./remote-agent-access/) | Reaching an agent on your LAN from anywhere without opening a port — a chat bot as the remote control, the three planes of the design, and the routing that makes your own hardware worth reaching | [README.md](./remote-agent-access/README.md) | [PATTERN.md](./remote-agent-access/PATTERN.md) · [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./remote-agent-access/IMPLEMENTATION.md) |","-| [Private Agent Network](./private-agent-network/) | A private network for an agent estate that spans machines — your agent's core in a cloud VPS, your files on a machine at home, neither able to reach the other. The tailnet that joins them, the one public listener that genuinely has to be public, the name trap that breaks webhook URLs silently, and the healthchecks that exercise the path instead of the process | [README.md](./private-agent-network/README.md) | [PATTERN.md](./private-agent-network/PATTERN.md) · [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./private-agent-network/IMPLEMENTATION.md) |","-| [Muse Patterns](./muse-patterns/) | Proactive planning for an agent — standing objectives that say when something is worth doing, trust scoring that decides which of them have earned the right to speak up, and a daily scan that reads your work history for new ones. The design question is not what an agent can notice but what it is allowed to interrupt you about | [README.md](./muse-patterns/README.md) | [PATTERN.md](./muse-patterns/PATTERN.md) · [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./muse-patterns/IMPLEMENTATION.md) |","-| [Tiiny guides](./Tiiny/) | Reference for the Tiiny Octo and the TiinyOS software it ships — every documentation surface the device exposes, how to tell the shipped documentation apart from your own data, where your API calls actually go, and what every model in the Model Store is actually good at | [README.md](./Tiiny/README.md) | [api-call-logging.md](./Tiiny/api-call-logging.md) · [model-catalogue.md](./Tiiny/model-catalogue.md) |","-","-More articles coming.","-","----","-","-## How to read","-","-Each article follows the same shape:","-","-- **README** — entry point, audience, quick-start","-- **PATTERN** — the portable pattern: why, what, design principles, failure modes. Read this if you want the mental model.","-- **IMPLEMENTATION** — the concrete setup: env vars, commands, prompts, shell scripts, end-to-end checklist. Read this if you're going to build it.","-- **scripts/** — runnable shell scripts you can copy and adapt.","-- **examples/** — worked examples of the pattern in practice, copyable as templates.","-","-If you only have ten minutes, read the article's README. If you have an hour, read README + PATTERN. If you're going to build it, also read IMPLEMENTATION and run the scripts.","-","-## Audience","-","-This repo is for people using AI agents to do professional work — partnerships, alliances, technical sales, product work, research — and finding the lack of inter-agent context painful.","-","-If you only run one agent on one computer, this repo is overkill. If you run two or more agents and want them to stop re-learning the same context every session, you'll find something useful here.","-","-## License","-","-- **Prose content** (READMEs, PATTERN.md, IMPLEMENTATION.md) — [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Free to share and adapt with attribution.","-- **Code and scripts** (`scripts/`, `examples/`) — [MIT License](./LICENSE). See `LICENSE` for full terms.","-","----","-","-*This is a personal publication. Articles represent current thinking and are subject to revision.*"]}],"deleted":true},{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.hermes-plugin-package-01-prune-repo-and-move-package-to-root/Tiiny/backup-with-restic.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":330,"newStart":0,"newLines":0,"lines":["-# Backing up a Tiiny AI Pocket Lab with restic","-","-A configuration guide for Tiiny owners: back up everything on the device","-that cannot be re-downloaded, to an external USB drive, with","-[restic](https://restic.net) — and leave the model weights out on purpose.","-","-Tested Nov 20, 2026, on TiinyOS 0.1.39. First backup: 8,897 files,","-3.29 GiB, 38 seconds, 2.38 GiB stored. Later runs are incremental and","-take seconds when nothing changed.","-","-## What this guide is not","-","-It assumes you already have shell access to your Tiiny with root","-available through `sudo`. That access comes from Tiiny's SSH access","-package, available from the makers of Tiiny — installing or enabling it","-is outside this guide. Nothing here covers Tiiny's proprietary software,","-the device's cloud account, or reinstalling TiinyOS.","-","-## Design decisions","-","-**Why restic.** The backup target is a USB drive, typically formatted","-exFAT. That rules out `rsync` with full fidelity (exFAT has no Unix","-permissions or ownership) and makes `tar` a poor fit (no deduplication,","-no incremental runs, no built-in verify). Restic is a single static","-binary, stores the repo as plain files on any filesystem, deduplicates","-and encrypts, and verifies with `restic check`. A plain tarball is fine","-for a one-off snapshot; restic is the right tool for a backup you run","-repeatedly.","-","-**Why model weights are excluded.** On the device this was tested on,","-the weights are ~720 GiB of an ~856 GiB data partition — the backup set","-without them is ~3.3 GiB. Weights are also the fastest-changing data on","-the device: models are added, removed, and updated frequently. And they","-are re-downloadable from their sources after a recovery. Including them","-would make every backup slow, huge, and dominated by data you can get","-back with a download — which means the backup would get run less often,","-which is the opposite of the point. What the backup covers instead is","-the irreplaceable part: configuration, app data, conversations, and","-service state. The weights are represented by a **manifest** (Section 3)","-that lists exactly what was installed, so recovery re-pulls the same","-models. The trade-off is explicit: recovery takes longer (re-download),","-but the backup itself is small and fast enough to run often. A backup","-you actually run beats a perfect one you never run.","-","-## 1. Mount the external drive","-","-Connect the drive to the Tiiny's USB port. The device sees it at the","-kernel level but does **not** auto-mount it, so mount it by hand.","-","-Find the partition:","-","-```","-lsblk -f","-blkid","-```","-","-Look for your drive (on the tested device it appeared as `/dev/sda`,","-with the data partition `/dev/sda2`, exFAT, label `Cdrive`). Then mount","-it:","-","-```","-sudo mkdir -p /media/Cdrive","-sudo mount -t exfat /dev/sda2 /media/Cdrive","-```","-","-Use your drive's own partition and label — `/dev/sda2` and `Cdrive`","-are examples. If your drive uses a different filesystem, use the","-matching `-t` type, or omit `-t` and let `mount` detect it. Verify:","-","-```","-df -h /media/Cdrive","-```","-","-You should see the drive's size and free space (the tested drive:","-3.7T total, 3.1T free). This mount does **not** survive a reboot —","-after restarting the device, mount it again before running a backup.","-Check that it is mounted before every run; the script in Section 4","-refuses to run if it is not.","-","-## 2. Install and configure restic","-","-**Install.** Download the Linux ARM64 release from restic's GitHub","-releases page and verify it against the published `SHA256SUMS` before","-using it:","-","-```","-# example — use the current release version","-curl -LO https://github.com/restic/restic/releases/download/v0.20.0/restic-0.20.0-linux-arm64.bz2","-# verify the checksum against the published SHA256SUMS, then:","-bzip2 -d restic-0.20.0-linux-arm64.bz2","-mkdir -p ~/bin","-cp restic-0.20.0-linux-arm64 ~/bin/restic","-chmod +x ~/bin/restic","-~/bin/restic version","-```","-","-**Repo password.** Restic encrypts the repo with a password. Keep it in","-a password file the script reads — never in the script itself. Create","-it once, typing the password invisibly:","-","-```","-read -s -p 'Restic password: ' P; echo; printf '%s' \"$P\" > ~/.restic-password && chmod 600 ~/.restic-password","-```","-","-Only you know this password. Losing it means the backup is","-irrecoverably lost; there is no reset.","-","-**Repo location.** The repo lives on the USB drive, so it travels with","-the drive and survives anything that happens to the device itself:","-","-```","-REPO=/media/Cdrive/restic-repo","-```","-","-The script initialises it on first run. It also keeps restic's cache on","-the drive (`/media/Cdrive/restic-cache`): if restic complains it cannot","-create its cache directory under `/root`, that is why — point","-`--cache-dir` at the drive, as the script does.","-","-## 3. The model manifest","-","-Because weights are excluded, each backup run regenerates a manifest —","-a text file listing what was installed, so recovery re-pulls the same","-models. It is written to `~/backup-manifest/manifest-<timestamp>.txt`,","-with `latest.txt` pointing at the newest, and a copy is kept on the","-drive under `/media/Cdrive/restic-manifests/`. Format:","-","-```","-Tiiny model manifest — 2026-11-20T140346-0800","-Models are EXCLUDED from the backup; re-pull by name after a recovery.","-","---- installed models (download_model) ---","-63G  /data/backend/tiiny_model_data/download_model/openai__gpt-oss-120b","-61G  /data/backend/tiiny_model_data/download_model/lightx2v__MiniMax-H3-Turbo","-42G  /data/backend/tiiny_model_data/download_model/Qwen__Qwen3-Coder-Next","-...","-","---- disk ---","-Filesystem  Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on","-/dev/...    858G  793G   22G  98% /data","-```","-","-One note: the timestamp in the filename has **no colons** — exFAT","-rejects `:` in filenames, and a colon-stamped filename fails to copy","-onto the drive.","-","-## 4. The backup script","-","-Save this as `~/bin/tiiny-backup.sh` and make it executable","-(`chmod +x ~/bin/tiiny-backup.sh`). It runs as root, checks its","-preconditions, regenerates the manifest, initialises the repo on first","-run, backs up, and verifies.","-","-```bash","-#!/bin/bash","-# Tiiny backup — restic. Model weights are excluded; a manifest is kept instead.","-#","-# Run as root on the device. The backup paths are root-owned, so run it","-# through sudo (see the SSH access documentation from Tiiny for how your","-# session reaches root). The repo password is never stored in this script:","-# it lives in a password file (mode 600) that you create once.","-#","-# Repo: <your USB drive>/restic-repo — restic stores its repo as plain","-# files, so any filesystem the drive uses is fine.","-#","-# Included: /data/backend minus model weights, Docker volumes,","-# /data/raw_data/upgrade minus bundles/cache, your home directory.","-# Excluded: model weights (re-downloadable; a manifest is kept instead),","-# Docker image layers, OTA bundles, image cache, ~/.cache.","-# First backup is a few GiB; later runs are incremental.","-set -euo pipefail","-","-if [ \"$(id -u)\" -ne 0 ]; then","-  echo \"run as root: sudo $0\"","-  exit 1","-fi","-","-RESTIC=/home/tiiny/bin/restic","-REPO=/media/Cdrive/restic-repo","-PWFILE=/home/tiiny/.restic-password","-# If restic cannot create its cache under /root, keep the cache on the","-# USB drive instead.","-CACHE_DIR=/media/Cdrive/restic-cache","-","-[ -x \"$RESTIC\" ] || { echo \"restic not found at $RESTIC\"; exit 1; }","-[ -f \"$PWFILE\" ] || { echo \"password file missing: $PWFILE (create it, mode 600)\"; exit 1; }","-mountpoint -q /media/Cdrive || { echo \"/media/Cdrive is not mounted\"; exit 1; }","-","-# --- Model manifest (what to re-pull after a recovery) ---","-MANIFEST_DIR=/home/tiiny/backup-manifest","-mkdir -p \"$MANIFEST_DIR\"","-STAMP=$(date +%Y-%m-%dT%H%M%S%z)","-{","-  echo \"Tiiny model manifest — $STAMP\"","-  echo \"Models are EXCLUDED from the backup; re-pull by name after a recovery.\"","-  echo","-  echo \"--- installed models (download_model) ---\"","-  du -sh /data/backend/tiiny_model_data/download_model/* 2>/dev/null | sort -rh || true","-  echo","-  echo \"--- disk ---\"","-  df -h /data /media/Cdrive","-} > \"$MANIFEST_DIR/manifest-$STAMP.txt\"","-ln -sf \"manifest-$STAMP.txt\" \"$MANIFEST_DIR/latest.txt\"","-# Note: the timestamp has no colons — exFAT rejects ':' in filenames.","-mkdir -p /media/Cdrive/restic-manifests","-cp \"$MANIFEST_DIR/manifest-$STAMP.txt\" /media/Cdrive/restic-manifests/","-","-# --- Init the repo on first run only ---","-if [ ! -f \"$REPO/config\" ]; then","-  \"$RESTIC\" --repo \"$REPO\" --password-file \"$PWFILE\" --cache-dir \"$CACHE_DIR\" init","-fi","-","-# --- Back up ---","-\"$RESTIC\" --repo \"$REPO\" --password-file \"$PWFILE\" --cache-dir \"$CACHE_DIR\" backup \\","-  --tag tiiny --tag no-models \\","-  --exclude /data/backend/tiiny_model_data \\","-  --exclude /data/raw_data/upgrade/os_blobs \\","-  --exclude /data/raw_data/upgrade/image-cache \\","-  --exclude /home/tiiny/.cache \\","-  /data/backend \\","-  /data/docker/volumes \\","-  /data/raw_data/upgrade \\","-  /home/tiiny","-","-# --- Verify ---","-\"$RESTIC\" --repo \"$REPO\" --password-file \"$PWFILE\" --cache-dir \"$CACHE_DIR\" snapshots --latest 1","-\"$RESTIC\" --repo \"$REPO\" --password-file \"$PWFILE\" --cache-dir \"$CACHE_DIR\" check","-","-echo","-echo \"--- done ---\"","-df -h /data /media/Cdrive","-du -sh \"$REPO\"","-```","-","-What is backed up, and what is not:","-","-| Included | Why |","-|---|---|","-| `/data/backend` (minus `tiiny_model_data`) | App state: webapp, conversation data, local databases |","-| `/data/docker/volumes` | Service state: configs, databases, workspace data |","-| `/data/raw_data/upgrade` (minus `os_blobs`, `image-cache`) | Update metadata worth keeping |","-| `/home/tiiny` (minus `.cache`) | User files and configuration |","-| Model manifest | What to re-pull after a recovery |","-","-| Excluded | Why |","-|---|---|","-| `/data/backend/tiiny_model_data` (~720 GiB) | Model weights — re-downloadable; see above |","-| Docker image layers (`overlay2`, `image`) | Rebuildable from images; large and churny |","-| OTA bundles, image cache | Superseded or re-fetchable update material |","-| `~/.cache` | Regenerable cache data |","-","-Adjust the path list to your device — the principle is the same: back","-up what you cannot re-download, exclude what you can.","-","-## 5. First run","-","-Mount the drive (Section 1), then run the script as root:","-","-```","-sudo ~/bin/tiiny-backup.sh","-```","-","-The first run initialises the repo, backs up, and verifies. Expect","-something like the tested run: 8,897 files, 3.29 GiB processed in","-38 seconds, 2.38 GiB stored (compression), one snapshot saved. The","-`restic check` at the end should report `no errors were found`.","-","-## 6. Verify and test restore","-","-`restic check` (run by the script every time) proves the repo is","-internally consistent. A test restore proves you can actually get data","-back — do it once after the first backup, and again any time the setup","-changes. Restore one small path to a temporary directory and compare","-it against the live original:","-","-```","-restic --repo /media/Cdrive/restic-repo --password-file ~/.restic-password \\","-  --cache-dir /media/Cdrive/restic-cache \\","-  restore latest --target /tmp/restore-test \\","-  --include /data/docker/volumes/aibox_connectors_config","-diff -r /tmp/restore-test/data/docker/volumes/aibox_connectors_config \\","-  /data/docker/volumes/aibox_connectors_config && echo DIFF_OK","-rm -rf /tmp/restore-test","-```","-","-On the tested device this restored 23 files (6.7 KiB) and the diff was","-identical. Restore into `/tmp`, never over live data, and remove the","-test directory afterward.","-","-## 7. Scheduling and retention","-","-Run the backup regularly — weekly is a reasonable cadence, and always","-after adding or removing models or changing configuration. The script","-as written keeps every snapshot; add retention once a schedule exists,","-for example keeping the last seven snapshots:","-","-```","-restic --repo /media/Cdrive/restic-repo --password-file ~/.restic-password \\","-  --cache-dir /media/Cdrive/restic-cache \\","-  forget --keep-last 7 --prune","-```","-","-Schedule it with whatever your setup provides (`cron` or a systemd","-timer). Keep the backup out of the device's busy hours if you run","-inference jobs on it.","-","-## 8. Recovering after a loss","-","-1. Reinstall TiinyOS on the device (Tiiny's own process) and get the","-   device back on the network.","-2. Re-attach the USB drive and mount it (Section 1).","-3. Re-pull the models listed in the latest manifest","-   (`/media/Cdrive/restic-manifests/manifest-<latest>.txt`).","-4. Restore the snapshot: `restic restore latest --target /` — or","-   restore individual paths first and check them, then the rest.","-5. Restart the device's services and confirm the apps come back.","-","-## Gotchas","-","-- **exFAT filenames:** no colons. Keep timestamps in filenames","-  colon-free (`2026-11-20T140346-0800`, not `14:03:46`).","-- **`--latest`, not `--last`:** restic 0.20.0 deprecated `--last`;","-  `snapshots --last 1` mis-parses and fails. Use `--latest 1`.","-- **Cache directory:** if restic cannot create its cache under","-  `/root`, use `--cache-dir` on the drive (Section 2).","-- **Mount before every run:** the drive does not auto-mount and the","-  mount does not survive a reboot. The script checks and stops if the","-  drive is not mounted.","-- **The repo password is the only key.** There is no reset. Keep it","-  somewhere safe, separate from the drive."]}],"deleted":true},{"f